SOAPBOX: My second callsign and operation in the contest. The North American
SOAPBOX: Record for SO QRP 15m was only 8 QSOs! Even I could beat that.
SOAPBOX: I configured my SO2R system for K6UFO on 10m and NN7SS on 15m.
SOAPBOX: I was somewhat limited in changing even/odd cycles by the SO2R
SOAPBOX: lockout, having to change both bands.
SOAPBOX: Aargh! Several times the software wouldn't keep my callsign
SOAPBOX: separate on 15m and reverted to K6UFO. I wasted at least an
SOAPBOX: hour of QSOs throughout the contest when I didn't notice quick
SOAPBOX: enough. Also the identification of Mults got messed up, probably
SOAPBOX: due to the same problem, so I wasn't able to see Mults well.
SOAPBOX: Operating from WA is not ideal for 10m or 15m. 10m managed 16 QSOs
SOAPBOX: in 7 hrs. 15m lasted longer and better with 79 QSOs in 9 hrs.
SOAPBOX: I worked anybody who could hear my QRP signals. I chased hundreds
SOAPBOX: of JAs and YBs with only a dozen successes. Don't ask about the
SOAPBOX: "rate." In the end, I beat my targets of "any" Qs on 10m and
SOAPBOX: "more than 8" Qs on 15m - but I don't think I'm winning any
SOAPBOX: plaques!
SOAPBOX: NN7SS (K6UFO)
SOAPBOX: 15m: GXP 3-el at 72ft
SOAPBOX: Flexradio 6600 at 5 watts
SOAPBOX: Writelog and DigiRite software
